WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.245 --> 00:00:00.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.452
HENGAMEH REZAEI: Karolis,
what are you doing?

00:00:03.452 --> 00:00:05.160
KAROLIS DINEIKA: I'm
trying to figure out

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.640
how I can automate all my lamps
and devices without manually

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.040
configuring each one.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.630
HENGAMEH REZAEI: Oh, I
have a solution for you.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:13.942
Just build some digital twins.

00:00:13.942 --> 00:00:15.150
KAROLIS DINEIKA: What's that?

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.910
HENGAMEH REZAEI: A digital
twin is like a digital version

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.050
of your physical devices.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.538
Let me show you how to build
one on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:23.538 --> 00:00:25.080
KAROLIS DINEIKA:
That sounds awesome.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.718
Let's get started.

00:00:27.718 --> 00:00:30.153
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.101 --> 00:00:35.720
HENGAMEH REZAEI: The solution
we build today looks like this.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.260
The blue boxes are
managed products,

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.580
which we can use directly.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:44.110
The only places where we
need to write our custom code

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:48.420
are the two yellow boxes, which
are Cloud Functions that links

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.105
IoT and Pub/Sub with Firestore.

00:00:52.105 --> 00:00:54.790
KAROLIS DINEIKA: So all the
infrastructure will be managed,

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.160
and we are just writing
some custom glue

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.765
to fit the pieces together.

00:00:58.765 --> 00:00:59.860
HENGAMEH REZAEI: Yes.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.810
Let me show you the code.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:05.319
For this project, we need
to have IoT Core set up.

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:07.840
You can find it in the console.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:13.270
I have created a registry, which
is a grouping of IoT devices.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.820
Under it, I have
gateway devices,

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.880
where our simulated
IoT device will be

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.470
talking to the gateway device.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.580
As you can see here, I
have my simulated device,

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.300
called lamp1, bounded
to the gateway.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.130
You can find a device by
clicking on devices as well.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:40.000
Once the lamp is connected, then
it will send events to IoT Core

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.570
every second.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:45.550
On event update, the event
will be sent from IoT Core

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:51.290
to Pub/Sub, and Pub/Sub triggers
the event to Cloud Functions.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.350
I also have another
function applying changes

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.100
from the user to the device.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.460
Let's look at the code.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.420
I use the device ID as
my reference for changes,

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:08.169
reading changes from Firestore
and eventually updating

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:10.780
the device with the latest info.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.770
Now, let's look at
the Firebase side.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.920
I made a collection of
devices with different states,

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.120
like ON and OFF.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.370
I also added the mode field
for brightness of each lamp.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.610
So now, in Cloud
Shell, I run my code.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.350
See what's happening
in Firestore?

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.630
I have added my
lamps, so you can

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:38.470
see the index is increasing
while each one is

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:42.550
sending their data
to IoT and Firestore.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:47.380
Let's change the mode of the
lamp, assuming you have an app,

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:51.040
and change it through that,
but I changed it here manually

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.360
to soft.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.810
The lamp mode immediately
changes to soft.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.860
This change is real time and
quickly handled by Firestore

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.370
for you.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.200
This is how you can build your
own digital twin solution.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.434
Try the lamps!

00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:08.854
[CLAPPING]

00:03:08.854 --> 00:03:10.480
KAROLIS DINEIKA: Wow, it works!

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.250
HENGAMEH REZAEI: Yes, indeed.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:16.215
Try out IoT Core and Firebase
for yourself at the link below.

00:03:16.215 --> 00:03:17.590
KAROLIS DINEIKA:
And don't forget

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:21.568
to subscribe for more great
Google Cloud Platform content.

00:03:21.568 --> 00:03:22.068
[CLAPPING]

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:24.060
[MACHINERY CLANKING]

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.180
As simple as that.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.720
Not even a compile error.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.760
HENGAMEH REZAEI: Now,
not even a compile error.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

